dna:['DNA double helix','BIOLOGY · MOLECULES','Two strands twist around each other, with base pairs linking them.',['Backbone|Sugar-phosphate rails of each strand.','Base pairs|A pairs with T; G pairs with C in DNA.'],'DNA stores hereditary information in base sequences.'],magnet:['Magnetic field & solenoid','ELECTROMAGNETISM · FIELD MODEL','Explore the field around a bar magnet and current-carrying coil. Field lines are a qualitative visualization, not individual physical threads.',['North pole|Outside the magnet, the conventional field direction is from north toward south.','South pole|Field lines return through the magnet to form closed loops.','Field line|Tangent direction indicates the local magnetic-field direction.','Solenoid|Current in a coil creates a field resembling a bar magnet.'],'Field strength depends on current, coil geometry, core material and distance.'],wave:['Transverse wave','WAVES · OSCILLATIONS','Observe a travelling transverse wave. The plotted line is displacement, not the path followed by the medium.',['Wave profile|Instantaneous displacement against position.','Crest|Maximum positive displacement from equilibrium.','Trough|Maximum negative displacement from equilibrium.','Equilibrium|The undisturbed reference line.'],'Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength: v = fλ.'],optics:['Convex lens & ray tracing','OPTICS · GEOMETRICAL MODEL','Trace parallel rays through a simplified converging lens. Thin-lens behavior is illustrated; real lenses have thickness and aberrations.',['Incident ray|Incoming light ray in a uniform medium.','Convex lens|A converging lens bends paraxial rays toward its principal axis.','Focal point|Ideal parallel paraxial rays converge near this point.','Refracted ray|Light changes direction at an optical boundary.'],'Thin-lens equation: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v using the chosen sign convention.'],cell:['Animal cell','BIOLOGY · CELL STRUCTURE','Inspect a simplified animal cell with a membrane, nucleus and organelles. Shapes and counts are schematic rather than to scale.',['Cell membrane|Selective boundary that regulates exchange with surroundings.','Nucleus|Contains most of the cell’s genetic material.','Mitochondrion|Site of much aerobic ATP production.','Endoplasmic reticulum|Membrane network involved in protein or lipid processing.','Golgi apparatus|Modifies, sorts and packages cellular products.'],'Cell structure varies by cell type; this is an educational overview, not a microscopy reconstruction.'],greenhouse:['Greenhouse effect','EARTH SYSTEM · ENERGY BALANCE','Explore incoming shortwave sunlight and outgoing infrared energy in a schematic atmosphere. Rays illustrate energy flows, not a literal scale drawing.',['Sunlight|Most incoming solar energy is shortwave radiation.','Surface|Absorbs energy and emits infrared radiation as it warms.','Atmosphere|Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared radiation.','Outgoing infrared|Energy ultimately escapes to space; the balance sets climate temperature.'],'This simplified energy-balance diagram is not a weather forecast.'],'orbital':['Quantum orbitals · s and p','CHEMISTRY · QUANTUM MODEL','Compare schematic s- and p-orbital probability shapes. These surfaces represent qualitative probability distributions, not classical electron paths.',['s orbital|Spherically symmetric probability density for an s state.','p orbital|Two-lobed angular probability shape with a nodal plane.','Nodal plane|A region where the ideal orbital wavefunction is zero.'],'Orbital shapes depend on quantum numbers; the model is a qualitative visualization, not a numerical wavefunction solver.'],
'thermo':['Heat engine · thermodynamics','THERMODYNAMICS · ENERGY','A conceptual heat engine transfers energy from a hot reservoir, produces work, and rejects waste heat to a cold reservoir.',['Hot reservoir|Supplies thermal energy Qₕ.','Working fluid|Undergoes a thermodynamic cycle in an idealized engine.','Work output|Net work is Qₕ − Q꜀ for a cyclic engine.','Cold reservoir|Receives rejected heat Q꜀.'],'For an ideal reversible engine, Carnot efficiency is η = 1 − T꜀/Tₕ with absolute temperatures. This is a schematic, not a cycle solver.'],
'electrolysis':['Water electrolysis','ELECTROCHEMISTRY · REDOX','An electrolyzer uses electrical energy to split water into hydrogen and oxygen. The schematic shows electrode products and electron flow qualitatively.',['Cathode|Reduction occurs here; hydrogen forms in an appropriate aqueous electrolyte.','Anode|Oxidation occurs here; oxygen forms in water electrolysis.','Electrolyte|Ionic conduction closes the circuit through the liquid.','Power supply|Electrical work drives a non-spontaneous overall reaction.'],'Overall: 2H₂O(l) → 2H₂(g) + O₂(g). Actual voltage and yield depend on overpotential, electrolyte and cell design.'],
'torque':['Torque & rotational equilibrium','MECHANICS · ROTATION','Explore how force and lever-arm distance set torque around a pivot. The model illustrates the vector relation τ = r × F.',['Pivot|Reference point for calculating torque.','Effort force|A perpendicular force produces torque magnitude rF.','Lever arm|Perpendicular distance from pivot to force line.','Load|Opposing torque in a simplified equilibrium.'],'Static equilibrium requires net force and net torque to be zero. The drawing is schematic.'],
'interference':['Wave interference','WAVES · SUPERPOSITION','Two coherent waves combine by superposition. In-phase waves reinforce; waves in opposite phase can cancel.',['Wave A|First sinusoidal displacement.','Wave B|Second sinusoidal displacement with adjustable phase in principle.','Resultant|Instantaneous sum of the two displacements.'],'This qualitative display illustrates superposition; no material medium or energy transport is numerically solved.'],
'kinetics':['Reaction energy profile','CHEMISTRY · KINETICS','Compare reactants, activation energy and products on a schematic reaction-coordinate diagram.',['Reactants|Starting chemical state.','Activation barrier|Energy required to reach the transition-state region.','Products|Final chemical state after reaction.'],'The curve is conceptual; real activation energies and reaction rates require measured or calculated chemistry data.'],
'stress':['Stress–strain response','MECHANICS · MATERIALS','Explore a schematic tensile stress–strain curve with an initial elastic region and a yield region.',['Elastic region|Deformation is approximately recoverable and stress is proportional to strain for linear elastic materials.','Yield point|Plastic deformation begins in a typical ductile material.','Ultimate strength|Peak engineering stress before necking/fracture in many ductile tests.'],'A generic schematic curve only; actual behavior depends on material, temperature, loading rate and specimen geometry.'],
'orbitalmotion':['Orbital mechanics','PHYSICS · GRAVITATION','A schematic central-gravity orbit demonstrates orbital motion around a massive body. It is not a precision ephemeris or numerical N-body integration.',['Central body|Dominant gravitating mass.','Orbiting body|Moves under idealized central gravity.','Orbit path|Illustrates an elliptical orbit.'],'For a two-body system, orbital behavior follows Newtonian gravity; this schematic is not intended for mission planning.'],
hydraulic:['Hydraulic press','FLUIDS · PASCAL’S PRINCIPLE','Explore force multiplication using two pistons connected by fluid. The moving parts are illustrative.',['Input piston|Applied force creates pressure in confined fluid.','Hydraulic fluid|Transmits pressure throughout the connected fluid.','Output piston|Larger area can produce larger force at lower displacement.','Cylinder|Constrains piston movement and contains the fluid.'],'Ideal relation: F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂; volume conservation means A₁d₁ = A₂d₂.']
